0.01335906982421875, -0.011749267578125, -0.0010538101196289062, -0.018463134765625, -0.00908660888671875, 0.006191253662109375, 0.0092315673828125, 0.00885772705078125, 0.0220184326171875, -0.0202178955078125, 0.01181793212890625, 0.027069091796875, 0.01116180419921875, 0.001270294189453125, -0.004459381103515625, 0.013580322265625, -0.01708984375, 0.024383544921875, -0.0270538330078125, -0.003265380859375, -0.0153350830078125, -0.0283355712890625, -0.006679534912109375, -0.01055908203125, 0.003604888916015625, 0.00772857666015625, 0.048858642578125, 0.015777587890625, 0.0114288330078125, -0.0264434814453125, -0.004444122314453125, 0.0098419189453125, 0.001903533935546875, 0.01160430908203125, 0.00205230712890625, -0.0249481201171875, 0.0072479248046875, -0.02264404296875, 0.0008120536804199219, -0.01038360595703125, -0.02142333984375, 0.0167999267578125, -0.043670654296875, -0.0060882568359375, -0.02593994140625, -0.019256591796875, 0.0169219970703125, -0.026947021484375, -0.0130462646484375, -0.01494598388671875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally improve their performance by storing and retrieving experiences in a textual memory bank\u2014a structured repository of text-based records summarizing past tasks, actions, and outcomes. These agents learn from an online stream of tasks, updating their memory to inform future decisions. Despite recent advances, the reliability and robustness of such agents remain underexplored. In this work, we address this gap by systematically evaluating the fragility\u2014defined here as the sensitivity of agent performance to stochasticity and task ordering\u2014of two representative memory-based self-improving methods.\n\nMethods\n\nWe focus on two recent memory-based agent architectures: Method A (a retrieval-augmented language model agent) and Method B (a memory-augmented reinforcement learning agent). Both agents operate in simulated environments involving multi-step reasoning tasks, such as sequential puzzle solving and navigation challenges. The agents' textual memory banks store summaries of previous task attempts, including actions taken, outcomes, and environment feedback.\n\nOur evaluation broadens prior work in two ways: (1) We conduct five independent runs per agent-task pair to quantify performance variance, and (2) we randomize task orderings across runs to assess the impact of curriculum effects. Performance is measured using task completion rate and average reward, with statistical significance assessed via paired t-tests (p < 0.05).\n\nResults\n\nOur experiments reveal two key findings. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy: for complex, multi-step tasks, the standard deviation of completion rates across runs is as high as 12% for Method A and 15% for Method B. Introducing the self-improving loop amplifies this variance, with some runs failing to improve over time. Second, agent improvement is highly dependent on task order. When tasks are presented in a random order, average completion rates drop by 18% compared to the default curriculum order, indicating a strong curriculum dependency.\n\nTo probe the causes of this fragility, we manually analyzed the agents' memory banks by sampling 50 memory entries per agent and categorizing them based on specificity, relevance, and completeness. We observed frequent underspecification: memory entries often lacked critical details about task requirements or environment constraints. This motivated the hypothesis that underspecified memories contribute to performance instability.\n\nTo validate this, we augmented the memory construction process with detailed rubrics and explicit environment feedback. Quantitatively, this intervention improved average completion rates by 9% (Method A) and 7% (Method B) under randomized task order, with improvements statistically significant (p < 0.05). However, a performance gap of 9\u201312% relative to the default curriculum order persisted, indicating that underspecification is only part of the problem.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe remaining performance gaps suggest that other factors\u2014such as memory retrieval errors and environment stochasticity\u2014also contribute to agent fragility. These gaps were measured as the difference in completion rates between the best-case (curriculum order with rubric-augmented memory) and worst-case (random order with standard memory) conditions. Our findings highlight the need for more robust memory representations and retrieval mechanisms.\n\nRecommendations\n\nWe recommend that future evaluations of memory-based self-improving agents: (1) report results across multiple independent runs to capture variance, (2) systematically randomize task order to assess curriculum effects, and (3) include ablations with enhanced memory specification (e.g., rubrics, explicit feedback). For human oversight, we suggest developing interfaces that allow users to inspect, edit, and annotate agent memories, enabling early detection of underspecification and other failure modes. These concrete steps will help ensure more reliable and interpretable self-improving agents."}}